WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.642
On a December afternoon in 1891, a young Canadian

2
00:00:03.642 --> 00:00:07.667
instructor nailed two peach baskets to the balcony rails of

3
00:00:07.667 --> 00:00:12.305
a Springfield gymnasium and handed his restless students a new game.

4
00:00:12.655 --> 00:00:16.022
He had invented basketball — and he was not yet an American.

5
00:00:16.372 --> 00:00:20.827
James Naismith was born on 6 November 1861 in Ramsay Township,

6
00:00:20.827 --> 00:00:25.497
in what was then Upper Canada, near the town of Almonte, Ontario.

7
00:00:25.847 --> 00:00:29.888
By the early 1890s he was a physical instructor at the

8
00:00:29.888 --> 00:00:34.752
International YMCA Training School in Springfield, Massachusetts.

9
00:00:35.102 --> 00:00:37.586
Two instructors had already given up on the class.

10
00:00:37.936 --> 00:00:40.142
Naismith was given two weeks to solve it.

11
00:00:40.492 --> 00:00:43.408
Naismith wanted a game of skill rather than brute force — one

12
00:00:43.408 --> 00:00:46.611
where you could not run with the ball or tackle the man who had it.

13
00:00:46.961 --> 00:00:49.541
He wrote out thirteen rules, asked a janitor for

14
00:00:49.541 --> 00:00:52.336
two boxes, and was handed two peach baskets instead.

15
00:00:52.686 --> 00:00:56.088
The first game, in December 1891, was played with

16
00:00:56.088 --> 00:00:59.629
a soccer ball and the baskets, nine players a side.

17
00:00:59.979 --> 00:01:01.326
It did not go smoothly.

18
00:01:01.676 --> 00:01:05.177
In January 1939, in an interview on the radio,

19
00:01:05.177 --> 00:01:09.059
James Naismith said this about the very first game:

20
00:01:09.659 --> 00:01:12.896
“The boys began tackling, kicking and punching in the clinches.

21
00:01:12.896 --> 00:01:16.184
They ended up in a free-for-all in the middle of the gym floor.”

22
00:01:16.784 --> 00:01:20.510
But the game spread with astonishing speed through

23
00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:24.609
the YMCA network, and the rules were published in 1892.

24
00:01:24.959 --> 00:01:27.618
Basketball was played at the 1936 Berlin

25
00:01:27.618 --> 00:01:30.277
Games, and Naismith was there to see it.

26
00:01:30.627 --> 00:01:34.672
He filed for American citizenship near the end of the First World

27
00:01:34.672 --> 00:01:38.593
War, and the grant came in 1925 — when he was sixty-three years

28
00:01:38.593 --> 00:01:42.887
old and had been teaching in the country for more than three decades.

29
00:01:43.237 --> 00:01:45.572
What makes someone belong to a country:

30
00:01:45.572 --> 00:01:48.206
the work they do there, or a piece of paper?
